      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/songs349/status/2055034189360373806",
      "text": "JPN media NHK🇯🇵 reports that JMOD is considering transferring its Type 88 SSMs to the Philippines🇵🇭 under Japan’s revised arms export regulations: https://t.co/wvKfso8ifO. The SSMs were live-fired by JGSDF troops during Exercise BALIKATAN 2026.\n\n■Translation:\n\nJMOD Considering Export of Surface-to-Ship Missiles to the Philippines\n\nIt has been learned that, regarding the Type 88 Surface-to-Ship Missile with a range of over one hundred kilometers operated by the Japan Ground Self-Defense Force (JGSDF), the Philippines expressed interest this month in receiving the equipment from Japan, prompting the JMOD to begin considering its export. \nLast month, Japan revised its policy to make the export of lethal “weapons” permissible in principle, and moves related to the overseas transfer of defense equipment are accelerating.\n\nThe “Type 88 Surface-to-Ship Missile” is a missile with a range of over one hundred kilometers capable of attacking naval vessels from land, and is operated by the JGSDF.\n\nAccording to interviews with relevant Japanese officials, the Philippines expressed interest this month in the transfer of the equipment from Japan, and the JMOD has begun considering exports of the missile.\n\nAs the missiles are scheduled to be gradually retired and replaced with newer models in the future, the government is currently considering exporting existing stockpiles.\n\nRegarding the “Type 88 Surface-to-Ship Missile,” the JSDF conducted live-fire exercises during the Exercises BALIKATAN with the U.S. military and others held in the Philippines from last month into this month.\n\nLast month, Japan revised the Three Principles on Transfer of Defense Equipment and Technology and their implementation guidelines, abolishing the so-called “five categories” and making the export of lethal “weapons” permissible in principle.\n\nJapan and the Philippines are also considering the transfer of the JMSDF's Abukuma-class destroyers, and moves related to the overseas transfer of defense equipment are accelerating.",
